{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V2vTrha85yTFXFRS3KXzYJ","type":"tool_result","content":"# ADR: Shared domain and optional worktree runtime\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted for the delivered scope — 2026-11-10 (proposed 2026-10-14, for B31/B33/B35). The\noptional worktree runtime (B35) was delivered in three slices, each reviewed independently, and\nthe last amendment below states what stands and where it departs from the decisions above:\na protected Unix-socket endpoint with identity and handshake, five hosted read checks with typed\nresults, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\nand needing a spec of their own before they are claimed: CPU quota and affinity, per-command\nmemory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\nPreserve accepted ADRs for active-context discovery, scoped Git readers, obligations and the\nMCP-Conductor-Harness validation boundary.\n\n## Context\n\nThe [performance analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md)\nseparates computation, transport and freshness costs. CLI-backed Store adapters\nspawn the binary with a fixed deadline; MCP persistence alone does not remove\nrepeated algorithms or graph copies. The [UX analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-07-operational-ux.md)\nshows plan/context composition and planning handoff gaps while P15 read parity\nalready works. The [consistency analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-09-consistency-recovery.md)\nidentifies nested mutable aliasing and generation/coordination boundaries.\n\nCached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\nper key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\nobservations, not measured corruption by a current consumer or a native race.\nA client-owned MCP stdio stream cannot be attached by an unrelated CLI. Existing\nsessions/cursors/registry mutexes do not prove worktree IPC or write serialization.\n\n## Decision\n\nPropose these boundaries for review, without choosing wire/schema details yet:\n\n- Extract one typed domain interface for admitted operations. CLI/MCP adapt syntax\n  and rendering, with common results/coverage/generation/error semantics. Preserve\n  the adapter-not-fork principle; remove the POSE subprocess only where extraction\n  is qualified. Legitimate scoped Git and external Harness execution remain separate.\n- Preserve project selection and actual authority/assurance. Read-only planning or\n  typed CLI handoff does not authorize writes; sharing code, process UID or stdio\n----\n9:results, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\n11:memory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\n25:Cached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\n26:per key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\n52:  inferred from a cache hit. Coordinate one production per key, budget retained\n69:  CPU quota/affinity, memory/IO/child-process and admission metrics. Protect short\n89:  parsing/copying/algorithms without establishing freshness or a memory budget.\n99:  risks memory growth and query starvation without a measured benefit.\n129:  concurrent productions, of queued keys (globally and per owner) and of waiters per\n130:  key is capped, a waiter that leaves gives its place back, and a caller refused for\n131:  lack of capacity gets `ErrCacheBusy` on every consumer: overload is an error the\n133:  few copy slots each, so a large copy never delays another key. The clone a caller\n134:  keeps after it returns is the caller's memory and is outside the budget; a reader\n137:  for it: they are the caller's memory, and bounding them is a recorded follow-up. A\n142:  lookup slots, 256 bytes), its key and its owner, so many small entries cannot exceed the\n146:- **Key.** Parsed graphs are keyed by engine version, schema and the digest of the\n149:  or modification time proves nothing. The test catalog is keyed by root and the\n154:  The design-delta report is keyed by root and the digest of its subject and\n155:  options, metered per worktree, assessed once per key, and retained only when\n158:- **Misses.** A miss is produced once per key however many callers ask together;\n159:  the production runs outside the lock, so one key never delays another. A waiter\n204:  and the server still holds the old one in memory (spec\n279:`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n296:- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n298:  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n301:  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n304:  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n305:  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n313 .pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"4204a4e2-0b96-42fc-9342-dbb7d545d797","timestamp":"2026-11-10T21:59:24.139Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ADR: Shared domain and optional worktree runtime\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted for the delivered scope — 2026-11-10 (proposed 2026-10-14, for B31/B33/B35). The\noptional worktree runtime (B35) was delivered in three slices, each reviewed independently, and\nthe last amendment below states what stands and where it departs from the decisions above:\na protected Unix-socket endpoint with identity and handshake, five hosted read checks with typed\nresults, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\nand needing a spec of their own before they are claimed: CPU quota and affinity, per-command\nmemory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\nPreserve accepted ADRs for active-context discovery, scoped Git readers, obligations and the\nMCP-Conductor-Harness validation boundary.\n\n## Context\n\nThe [performance analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md)\nseparates computation, transport and freshness costs. CLI-backed Store adapters\nspawn the binary with a fixed deadline; MCP persistence alone does not remove\nrepeated algorithms or graph copies. The [UX analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-07-operational-ux.md)\nshows plan/context composition and planning handoff gaps while P15 read parity\nalready works. The [consistency analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-09-consistency-recovery.md)\nidentifies nested mutable aliasing and generation/coordination boundaries.\n\nCached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\nper key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\nobservations, not measured corruption by a current consumer or a native race.\nA client-owned MCP stdio stream cannot be attached by an unrelated CLI. Existing\nsessions/cursors/registry mutexes do not prove worktree IPC or write serialization.\n\n## Decision\n\nPropose these boundaries for review, without choosing wire/schema details yet:\n\n- Extract one typed domain interface for admitted operations. CLI/MCP adapt syntax\n  and rendering, with common results/coverage/generation/error semantics. Preserve\n  the adapter-not-fork principle; remove the POSE subprocess only where extraction\n  is qualified. Legitimate scoped Git and external Harness execution remain separate.\n- Preserve project selection and actual authority/assurance. Read-only planning or\n  typed CLI handoff does not authorize writes; sharing code, process UID or stdio\n----\n9:results, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\n11:memory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\n25:Cached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\n26:per key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\n52:  inferred from a cache hit. Coordinate one production per key, budget retained\n69:  CPU quota/affinity, memory/IO/child-process and admission metrics. Protect short\n89:  parsing/copying/algorithms without establishing freshness or a memory budget.\n99:  risks memory growth and query starvation without a measured benefit.\n129:  concurrent productions, of queued keys (globally and per owner) and of waiters per\n130:  key is capped, a waiter that leaves gives its place back, and a caller refused for\n131:  lack of capacity gets `ErrCacheBusy` on every consumer: overload is an error the\n133:  few copy slots each, so a large copy never delays another key. The clone a caller\n134:  keeps after it returns is the caller's memory and is outside the budget; a reader\n137:  for it: they are the caller's memory, and bounding them is a recorded follow-up. A\n142:  lookup slots, 256 bytes), its key and its owner, so many small entries cannot exceed the\n146:- **Key.** Parsed graphs are keyed by engine version, schema and the digest of the\n149:  or modification time proves nothing. The test catalog is keyed by root and the\n154:  The design-delta report is keyed by root and the digest of its subject and\n155:  options, metered per worktree, assessed once per key, and retained only when\n158:- **Misses.** A miss is produced once per key however many callers ask together;\n159:  the production runs outside the lock, so one key never delays another. A waiter\n204:  and the server still holds the old one in memory (spec\n279:`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n296:- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n298:  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n301:  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n304:  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n305:  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n313 .pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01V2vTrha85yTFXFRS3KXzYJ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"subcommandResults"}}]}
